WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.440
The key issue is not
deliberate or not deliberate.

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:07.170
Once you've decided to learn a language,
you're gonna take some deliberate steps

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:09.360
to, you know, acquire that language.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:09.960
Hi there.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:15.450
Steve Kaufmann here uh, and today I want
to talk about, you know, how deliberate

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:17.400
should we be in learning a language?

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:22.439
Should we deliberately study the language
or do we just acquire it naturally?

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:27.330
Uh, remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:28.949
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:28.949 --> 00:00:32.670
If you are following me on a podcast
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:34.620
So this subject comes up.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:39.030
You hear people say, well, you know,
we just learned the way kids do.

00:00:39.464 --> 00:00:43.245
Uh, obviously children, especially
say young children that move to

00:00:43.245 --> 00:00:46.785
a new country uh, they acquire
the language quite naturally.

00:00:46.785 --> 00:00:53.324
They don't study, they don't deliberately
try to learn it, uh, and sort of, um,

00:00:53.385 --> 00:00:56.804
compelling input, if we're driven by
our interest in the subject matter,

00:00:56.804 --> 00:01:02.385
we're not so conscious let's say of
sort of a deliberate effort to learn.

00:01:02.745 --> 00:01:06.134
Certainly in my experience now
with Persian, where I had a lot of

00:01:06.134 --> 00:01:11.235
interesting content I was mostly
pursuing this sort of interesting

00:01:11.235 --> 00:01:16.425
content about Iran and I wasn't sort
of consciously learning, but, but is it

00:01:16.425 --> 00:01:18.435
true that we don't deliberately learn?

00:01:18.555 --> 00:01:20.535
I think we do as adults.

00:01:20.565 --> 00:01:23.955
If we decide to learn a
language, we make a decision.

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:26.565
We are deliberately deciding to learn.

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:31.289
So, uh, we're not like the child who's
surrounded by friends and will gradually

00:01:31.289 --> 00:01:32.850
and naturally pick up the language.

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:38.520
Also, if we don't do something
quite deliberate just by being

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.070
in the environment, we aren't
going to learn the language.

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:44.160
We have, uh, Spanish on, uh,
in the background, Spanish

00:01:44.164 --> 00:01:45.539
radio while we're working.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:48.840
And we don't understand anything
of what we're listening to.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:53.090
We're unlikely to acquire very much of
the language or it would take forever

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:54.680
to acquire the language that way.

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:59.780
Typically again, if a person moves to
a country where that person would like

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:03.440
to learn the language, whether that
be France or Japan or places where I

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:09.605
have lived, the, whether it be expat
or if, you know, immigrant or whatever

00:02:09.605 --> 00:02:13.265
has to make a deliberate effort to
learn the language, the language

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:15.665
is not just gonna come naturally.

00:02:16.145 --> 00:02:20.255
So then the question is, you
know, how deliberate should

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:22.135
our learning activities be?

00:02:22.925 --> 00:02:27.185
So at one extreme, you could say
that if, if you have this deliberate

00:02:27.185 --> 00:02:30.760
learning activity, which consists
of reading the dictionary, that's

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.280
probably not gonna get you too far.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:38.950
In other words, it's gonna take forever
to be able to use those words and

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:40.960
to acquire vocabulary in that way.

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:45.910
If you have a deliberate learning
activity, such as say, doing a

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:50.980
lot of grammatical exercises and
studying grammar, uh, I think it

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:53.980
depends on the person, but in my
own view, I think it would take a

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:55.480
long time to learn the language.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.060
When we learn the language, we're
actually trying to acquire new habits.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:05.730
So there is this aspect of the sort of
natural acquisition that in the case of

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:10.320
young children who are uninhibited, who
are surrounded by their friends, speaking

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:14.400
the language, young, you know, children,
they don't care about making mistakes.

00:03:14.405 --> 00:03:16.260
They gradually correct their own mistakes.

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:18.880
The brain develops these new habits.

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:22.600
They don't really need any correction.

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:26.140
You know, I, I, I think it's kind of
amusing when I hear people say that the

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:30.310
mother corrects the child in learning,
uh, you know, their first language.

00:03:30.315 --> 00:03:31.450
That's simply not true.

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:36.459
Uh, because again, Uh, the, uh,
children of immigrants, even if the

00:03:36.459 --> 00:03:39.880
parents can't speak the language,
the children acquire it very quickly.

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:44.049
So it's not true that the parents,
you know, correct their children

00:03:44.049 --> 00:03:47.260
and therefore the children learn
the ch children learn naturally.

00:03:47.739 --> 00:03:49.899
But as adults, it's a
little more difficult.

00:03:49.899 --> 00:03:52.690
We're not as uninhibited as the children.

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:55.660
We don't get as much
exposure as the children get.

00:03:55.869 --> 00:04:00.185
So we have to have some degree
of deliberate study, but

00:04:00.335 --> 00:04:01.925
how deliberate should it be?

00:04:02.165 --> 00:04:06.275
In other words, uh, doing
grammar exercises personally,

00:04:06.275 --> 00:04:07.595
I find uninteresting.

00:04:07.595 --> 00:04:11.225
So, uh, I, I don't find any meaning there.

00:04:11.225 --> 00:04:13.295
I don't find that to be
a meaningful activity.

00:04:13.535 --> 00:04:16.925
I don't find reading the dictionary
to be a meaningful activity.

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:21.920
however, if I'm starting into a
language, uh, using our mini stories

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:23.900
at LingQ, I find that to be meaningful.

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:27.950
It's a story, not a tremendously
captivating story, but it's a story.

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:29.600
So there is some meaning there.

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:35.180
I can spend some months on the mini
stories because it is meaningful to me in

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:39.170
the sense that I have deliberately decided
that I want to acquire this language.

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:44.415
So through repetitive listening and
reading I acquire enough vocabulary

00:04:44.475 --> 00:04:48.645
that I can eventually start
exploring more meaningful content.

00:04:49.095 --> 00:04:53.035
And so, so to me, the key issue is
not deliberate or not deliberate...

00:04:53.205 --> 00:04:57.765
once you've decided to learn a language,
you're gonna take some deliberate steps

00:04:57.855 --> 00:04:59.925
to, you know, acquire that language.

00:04:59.955 --> 00:05:04.044
And the key thing there is meaning,
and, and this gets back to what Dr.

00:05:04.044 --> 00:05:08.445
Krashen always saya: we learn the language
from meaningful messages where we're

00:05:08.445 --> 00:05:12.975
motivated to understand the message,
but there again, if we go back to the

00:05:12.975 --> 00:05:17.055
example of just listening to Spanish
in the background, if you can't, if you

00:05:17.055 --> 00:05:22.305
have no way of trying to understand what
is being said, then it's gonna be very

00:05:22.305 --> 00:05:24.285
difficult to learn from that experience.

00:05:24.615 --> 00:05:28.914
That's why, when I listen, I like to have
access to a transcript so that I have a

00:05:28.914 --> 00:05:33.505
chance of understanding of, of finding
meaning in what it is I'm listening to.

00:05:33.655 --> 00:05:38.305
It doesn't have to be a hundred percent
meaning, but there has to be some meaning.

00:05:38.724 --> 00:05:41.455
And, uh, so that, to that
extent, it's deliberate.

00:05:41.635 --> 00:05:44.515
Now, do we need correction for example?

00:05:44.515 --> 00:05:46.765
So there's another deliberate strategy.

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:48.414
You get a teacher who corrects you.

00:05:48.775 --> 00:05:51.145
Personally I find that
that's not necessary.

00:05:51.534 --> 00:05:56.044
It's certainly possible that when I'm
fairly advanced in the language there

00:05:56.044 --> 00:05:58.830
are errors that I will continue to make.

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:03.840
And if someone were to correct me, I
might stop making those errors maybe,

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:09.180
but presumably I will develop certain
habits that it will be difficult for me

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:13.980
to correct, but it won't dramatically
inhibit my communication in the language.

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:17.100
So on the question of,
do we need correction?

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:22.110
I would basically tend to come down on
the side of, we don't need correction.

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:24.840
If you get corrected fine,
but we don't need it.

00:06:26.135 --> 00:06:30.635
Corrections can inhibit this other
thing that's so important for language

00:06:30.635 --> 00:06:32.315
acquisition that is confidence.

00:06:32.554 --> 00:06:37.835
So if I'm, uh, say I'm, uh, visiting a
country, I don't know a Czech Republic

00:06:37.895 --> 00:06:42.185
and I go in a bookstore and, uh,
in my best Czech I ask something

00:06:42.224 --> 00:06:46.039
and the sales clerk there begins
my by correcting my Czech.

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:49.095
Or, you know, Chinese
or whatever it might be.

00:06:49.725 --> 00:06:53.865
If that happens a couple of
times, then I'm gonna be less

00:06:53.865 --> 00:06:56.145
willing to speak in the language.

00:06:56.295 --> 00:07:00.405
Whereas if the, if the sales clerk,
you know, needn't be Czech, it can

00:07:00.405 --> 00:07:01.695
be Portuguese, it could be anything.

00:07:01.755 --> 00:07:06.195
And the sales clerk immediately
understands what I'm saying and, uh,

00:07:06.195 --> 00:07:08.505
answers my question then I'm encouraged.

00:07:08.505 --> 00:07:12.645
And so that builds up my confidence,
uh, and builds up my fluency.

00:07:12.645 --> 00:07:17.205
So I, I would tend to say that
if correction is, is a form of

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:20.985
deliberate instruction, then I
think correction is not a good idea.

00:07:21.405 --> 00:07:25.545
Uh, I think, uh, I've said this
before, comprehension questions are

00:07:25.545 --> 00:07:30.495
not a good idea because it spoils the
fun, uh, of listening and reading.

00:07:31.105 --> 00:07:34.835
I, I, I was, uh, thinking the other
day, you know, when I read, uh, you

00:07:34.835 --> 00:07:39.155
know, Treasure Island by, uh, Robert
Lewis Stevenson, there were terms in

00:07:39.155 --> 00:07:40.590
there I didn't understand Jacob...

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:42.425
Jacob..., I didn't know what that was.

00:07:42.425 --> 00:07:46.325
I'm still not entirely clear on
the history of that period in, in

00:07:46.325 --> 00:07:49.955
Scotland, but it doesn't matter, you
know, you can, you can understand

00:07:49.955 --> 00:07:52.295
60%, 70% and still enjoy it.

00:07:52.625 --> 00:07:56.975
So comprehen comprehension
questions, no, I'm not in favor of.

00:07:57.455 --> 00:08:02.900
Uh, A lot of the sort of deliberate
learning strategy, teaching strategy

00:08:02.930 --> 00:08:08.150
that is used in schools I'm not really
in favor of, but it's not true to say

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:10.640
that we don't have a deliberate strategy.

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:15.230
And I think it's up to every person
to find the strategy that suits them.

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:21.980
And it'll be sort of a mixture of, uh,
what I would call the sort of big picture.

00:08:22.844 --> 00:08:26.115
Which is listening and reading
and, and engaging with the language

00:08:26.205 --> 00:08:27.705
and eventually watching movies.

00:08:27.705 --> 00:08:30.945
And, and you're getting that whole
language into you and getting

00:08:30.950 --> 00:08:32.505
used to it, to, to the language.

00:08:32.564 --> 00:08:34.515
And that's the big picture learning.

00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:38.820
But you probably need to spend a little
bit of time on the nuts and bolts.

00:08:39.090 --> 00:08:46.740
So I have, uh, you know, regularly refer
to grammars uh, it's thin grammars that

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:48.930
I found on the internet that I download.

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:52.290
And I refer to them when I
already have some understanding

00:08:52.295 --> 00:08:53.790
of how the language works.

00:08:53.795 --> 00:08:55.920
I might have a quick passover initialy.

00:08:56.175 --> 00:08:59.625
But then I, I find myself going
back and going back because there

00:08:59.625 --> 00:09:03.015
are things that I notice in the
language that I don't fully understand

00:09:03.015 --> 00:09:04.665
how they work and I look them up.

00:09:04.755 --> 00:09:10.425
So that, to some extent, some emphasis
or some, you know, looking at deliberate

00:09:10.425 --> 00:09:13.974
study of the nuts and bolts, you
know, is a worthwhile thing to do.

00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:15.940
So is that 80/20?

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:17.350
I don't know, 90/10?

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:21.790
It's sort of a part of it is that
overall big picture, getting the language

00:09:21.790 --> 00:09:25.600
in you getting the brand used to the
language, but there is some deliberate,

00:09:25.750 --> 00:09:28.750
I mean, not the least of which is
looking up words when I'm on link.

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:29.500
I look up a word.

00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:30.190
I don't know what it means.

00:09:30.190 --> 00:09:30.670
I have to look it up.

00:09:31.199 --> 00:09:34.319
So there are various deliberate
learning activities that,

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:36.000
that we need to indulge in.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:40.770
And of course, the overall decision to
learn a language as an adult adult is a

00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:45.240
deliberate decision, uh, which is, as I
said, not the case with children who are

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:49.020
simply, you know, at the age of five,
they're put into an environment and,

00:09:49.170 --> 00:09:51.930
and hardly noticed that all of a sudden
they're speaking the local language.

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:53.835
So deliberate.

00:09:54.075 --> 00:09:56.355
We make a deliberate
decision to learn a language.

00:09:56.625 --> 00:10:02.895
We make a deliberate decision to stay
with it, uh, and, uh, hopefully move

00:10:02.895 --> 00:10:04.725
to where there's more and more meaning.

00:10:04.725 --> 00:10:06.875
And it seems more and more natural.

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:12.090
It seems to acquire less effort and we
gradually start to acquire the language.

00:10:12.390 --> 00:10:16.770
So, uh, I have dealt with this
subject of the details versus the,

00:10:17.250 --> 00:10:21.480
you know, getting the language in
you through input, uh, previously.

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:24.420
And I found two videos that
are almost 10 years old.

00:10:24.425 --> 00:10:28.980
So if you're interested, you can have
a look to see whether I contradicted

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:30.780
today what I said 10 years ago.

00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:31.740
Thanks for listening.

00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:32.260
Bye for now.

